# REGRESSION — an `on error:` handler inside a `do:` block must reach the
# liveness seed.
#
# `on_error_bodies_in` was exhaustive over `Stmt` and BLIND TO `Expr`.
# `Expr::Do` and `Expr::Block` carry statement blocks, so a handler
# registered inside a `do:` -- as a statement, or as a VarDecl value --
# was unreachable and the seed was provably INERT on it: ablating the
# seed left this shape GIR byte-identical while a control moved 85
# lines. rc 0 GARBAGE on C and LLVM at the commit before the fix.
#
# The expression half now routes through visitor::visit_expr_children,
# the one exhaustive child enumeration, rather than being hand-rolled a
# second time. The guard row that pinned this walker asserted the very
# property this fixture disproves.
#
# The `v.set(0, ...)` is load-bearing: it frees the value moved into
# the vector.
# PROBE D1 -- pass 15/16's A_after_reg.gg with the `on error:` registration
# wrapped in a `do:` block. ONE thing varied vs the in-tree GREEN fixture
# tests/fixtures/liveness_on_error_after_registration.gg: the wrapper.
# The v.set(0, ...) is load-bearing -- it frees the value moved into the vector.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

int f(int n) throws String:
    String s = mk("abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ")
    do:
        on error:
            print(s)
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(s)
    v.set(0, mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
    if n > 0:
        throw "boom"
    return 1

void main():
    int r = f(1)! catch (e): -1
    print(r)
